AB  - The increasing demand and widespread use of honey underscore its importance as an essential and unique natural product. Ensuring its authenticity and nutritional quality requires a detailed understanding of botanical origin, storage conditions, and potential compositional changes. In this context, expanding physicochemical analyses becomes vital to prevent adulteration and maintain quality control. This study examines the application of near-infrared (NIR) spectroscopy, combined with chemometric modelling, for rapid screening of Bulgarian honey. A total of 108 samples, including acacia, linden, rapeseed, sunflower, and polyfloral varieties, were collected from different regions across Bulgaria. Five physicochemical parameters were measured: active acidity, sugar content (glucose and fructose), electrical conductivity, refractive index, and water content. Spectral data were captured in the $900-1700 ~\text{nm}$ range using the NIRQuest 512 spectrometer. Partial Least Squares (PLS) regression models were developed for each parameter, applying various spectral preprocessing techniques. The most accurate predictions were achieved using first derivative transformations combined with autoscaling or mean-centring, depending on the botanical origin. For instance, the model for pH content in monofloral honey achieved a cross-validation correlation coefficient of 0.95, whereas for polyfloral honey, it was 0.78. Similarly, water content and refractive index were predicted with high reliability, with $\mathrm{r}_{\text{cv}}$ values of 0.83 for polyfloral honey and 0.79 for monofloral honey. Electrical conductivity and sugar content (glucose and fructose) also demonstrated strong predictive performance, especially in polyfloral samples ($\mathrm{r}_{\text{cv}}=0.86$). These findings confirm the potential of NIR spectroscopy, combined with PLS regression, as a quick, non-destructive, and reliable method for evaluating the physicochemical profile of honey.
ER  - 

