AB  - This study examined the effects of convective solar drying on the physiochemical quality of hot peppers harvested in Algeria. The fresh products were dried convectively until reaching a desired stable weight. The moisture content, water activity, pH, crude protein, crude fat, total sugars, and polyphenol content were measured. Using an ideal drying method, it required between 2 and 5 days with Aw values of (67, 59) of moisture content, (0.26) of ash, (4.1), PH, and acidity levels of (2.8), Ash levels of (32.41), Dry matter of (73.38), the total protein content of (0.83), the sugar content of (2.9), and polyphenol content of (89) and crude fat content of (0.23).in contrast, the values of fresh Hot pepper were (89),(0.92),(5.34),(3.6),(11),(8),(1.64), and (1.64),(125),(0.36) respectively. The observed results confirmed that the solar drying protected the nutritional value of this product with few losses when compared to the values of the harvested fresh product
